        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mississippian culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> valley and its tributaries and the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> and from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> to Georgia, the Carolinas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 800–1600 CE: Emergent Mississippian villages from about 800; Cahokia’s rise about 1050; regional centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moundville-archaeological-site">Moundville</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etowah-indian-mounds">Etowah</a> from the 12th to the 16th centuries; the last chiefdoms met by Spanish expeditions in 1539–1568</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Towns of earthen platform mounds around plazas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monks-mound">Monks Mound</a> at Cahokia the largest; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming; chiefdoms ruled by hereditary elites; shell-tempered pottery; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, shell and stone art of the Southeastern Ceremonial Complex</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations at Cahokia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moundville-archaeological-site">Moundville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etowah-indian-mounds">Etowah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiro-mounds">Spiro</a>, Lake Jackson and hundreds of other sites; the chronicles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a>’s expedition by the Gentleman of Elvas, Rodrigo Rangel, Luys Hernández de Biedma and Garcilaso de la Vega; the records of Juan Pardo’s expeditions; French accounts of the Natchez; the traditions of descendant nations</span></div>
        </div>
