    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Mississippian culture was a way of life shared from about 800 to 1600 by many Native American peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> valley and the South-East, built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming and hereditary chiefs.</li><li>Its towns centred on earthen platform mounds and plazas, from Cahokia, the largest, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moundville-archaeological-site">Moundville</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etowah-indian-mounds">Etowah</a> in Georgia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiro-mounds">Spiro</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</li><li>Spanish expeditions from 1539 met its last great chiefdoms; after epidemics, wars and slave raids their peoples regrouped as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>, Choctaw, Chickasaw and other nations.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Chunkey, a game in which players threw poles after a rolling stone disc, spread from the Cahokia region with Mississippian ways; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>, Choctaw and Chickasaw men still played it in the 18th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> have revived it.</p></aside>
    </section>

